---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import { basePath, siteUrl } from '../lib/site';

const title = 'Image Tools - Free Online Image Compressor and Converter | FreeKit';
const description =
  'Free online image tools: compress images with a quality slider, convert between PNG, JPEG, WebP, and AVIF. No signup, no watermark. Everything runs in your browser.';

const tools = [
  { href: '/image-compressor', icon: 'compress', name: 'Image compressor', desc: 'Shrink photos and screenshots with a quality slider, batch and ZIP included.' },
  { href: '/image-converter', icon: 'photo', name: 'Image converter', desc: 'Convert between PNG, JPEG, WebP, and AVIF, with a transparency warning for JPEG.' },
] as const;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: 'FreeKit image tools',
  url: siteUrl(Astro.site) + basePath('/image-tools'),
  description,
};
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="page-head">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={basePath("/")}>FreeKit</a><span><span class="sep">/</span><a href={basePath("/image-tools")}>Image tools</a></span>
      </nav>
      <h1>Image tools</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Two utilities for everyday image jobs: shrinking files with a quality slider you control,
        and converting between the formats your software and websites actually accept.
        All free, all private, all in your browser.
      </p>
      <div>
        <span class="privacy-line"><Icon name="lock" size={15} /> Your files never leave your device.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Ad slot: hub top (AdSense display unit goes here) -->
    <div class="ad-slot" data-ad-slot="hub-top" aria-hidden="true"><span>Advertisement</span></div>

    <div class="tool-index">
      {tools.map((t) => (
        <a class="tool-row" href={basePath(t.href)}>
          <span class="tool-row-icon"><Icon name={t.icon} size={20} /></span>
          <span>
            <span class="tool-row-name">{t.name}</span>
            <p class="tool-row-desc">{t.desc}</p>
          </span>
          <span class="arrow"><Icon name="arrow" size={18} /></span>
        </a>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="content">
      <h2>What these tools do</h2>
      <p>
        Most image work is not editing. It is preparing: a photo that is too big to email, a WebP
        that your software cannot open, a BMP scan eating disk space for no reason. The compressor
        handles the first job with a quality slider and per-file before/after sizes, so you can see
        exactly what each setting costs. The converter handles the rest, redrawing each image in the
        format you pick.
      </p>
      <p>
        A useful pattern: run heavy photos through <a href={basePath("/image-compressor")}>Image compressor</a>
        first at quality 80, then use <a href={basePath("/image-converter")}>Image converter</a> only when the
        format itself is the problem, such as a PNG that needs to become a JPEG for an upload form. Both tools
        process batches and hand you a single ZIP at the end. Everything stays on your device the whole way through.
      </p>

      <h2>Honest limitations, stated up front</h2>
      <p>
        PNG is lossless, so compressing a PNG as PNG barely changes its size; real savings come from converting
        to JPEG or WebP, and both tools say so where it matters. JPEG has no transparency, so converting a
        transparent image to JPEG forces a background color: the tools warn you and offer a white fill instead of
        silently turning those areas black. AVIF output appears only when your browser can actually encode it.
        Animated GIFs convert as a single still frame.
      </p>

      <h2>Common questions</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="faq">
          <dt>Are these image tools free?</dt>
          <dd>Yes. Both tools are free with no limits, no watermarks, and no account.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="faq">
          <dt>Do my images get uploaded?</dt>
          <dd>No. Both tools process your files locally in the browser. Nothing is sent to a server, which is why they also work offline once loaded.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="faq">
          <dt>Will my images lose quality?</dt>
          <dd>It depends on the settings. JPEG, WebP, and AVIF outputs are lossy and discard some detail; PNG output is lossless. The compressor shows the size saving per file so you can judge the trade-off, and nothing is final until you download.</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="faq">
          <dt>What about transparency?</dt>
          <dd>PNG, WebP, and AVIF keep the alpha channel. JPEG cannot store transparency, so both tools warn you when an image actually has transparent pixels and offer a white background fill for JPEG output.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <!-- Ad slot: hub bottom (AdSense display unit goes here) -->
    <div class="ad-slot" data-ad-slot="hub-bottom" aria-hidden="true"><span>Advertisement</span></div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
